#limitation(title: [Handgrip Strength Cannot Stand Alone as a Diagnostic Test])[
The same studies that show a group-level HGS deficit also show that HGS does not separate ME/CFS from other fatiguing conditions at the individual level.

- In the pediatric/young-adult cohort, *no* HGS index significantly separated patients classified as noME/CFS from those classified as ME/CFS. Across the three-group model (healthy control, noME/CFS, ME/CFS), accuracy was 49.2–57.3% against a no-information rate of 36.5%. Only in the CCC-restricted sensitivity analysis did discrimination reach moderate levels (accuracy 62.8–70.7%; C-statistic 0.63–0.73) @Mihatsch2026HandgripCYP.
- In the post-COVID cohort, HGS separated patients with and without ME/CFS with AUC 0.61–0.63 (Fmean, fatigue ratio, recovery ratio) @Wallraven2026HandgripPCS.
- In a machine-learning comparison of functional measures in post-COVID patients, fatigue ranked first in importance while grip strength ranked fifth, and the cohort reached only 23% of the sample size required for the eight-parameter model @DazaArana2026ML.

The reported positive predictive value of 55.6% @Mihatsch2026HandgripCYP is cohort-specific: it depends on the local pre-test probability (here, a tertiary referral population where 57% had ME/CFS) and cannot be transported to primary care without recalibration.

Three further constraints limit transportability. *Cohort overlap:* the adult HGS cohort (105 patients, 66 controls) and its variability analysis are the same study population, so they count as one independent source, not two @Jakel2021HGS @Popkirov2025CV. *Protocol heterogeneity:* grip count, dynamometer model, rest intervals, and the definition of the recovery ratio differ across the three cohorts, so no single threshold can be pooled. *Population mixing:* the pediatric/young-adult, adult ME/CFS, and post-COVID cohorts differ in age, diagnostic criteria, and illness duration, and subgroup estimates within them are small.

*Certainty: 0.60* for the limitation (direct, consistent findings across two independent cohorts).

*Consequence:* Handgrip testing cannot rule ME/CFS out, and a normal result should not be read as evidence against the diagnosis — it is a severity and trajectory measure, not a diagnostic test.
] <lim:handgrip-diagnostic-discrimination>
